  "text": {
    "tagline": "Eight seconds of tape behind every key.",
    "blurb": "For sixty years our workshop has kept tape-replay keyboards playing. This disc puts one in your computer: {count} voices of strings, flutes, choirs, brass and organs, each with eight seconds of tape behind the key, and the wow, flutter and start-up scoop of a well-loved machine.",
    "intro": [
      "Welcome to the Tape Parlour Ensemble, Restored Edition. Behind every key of a tape-replay keyboard is a strip of tape about eight seconds long. Press the key and the tape is drawn across a head; hold it past eight seconds and the note simply ends.",
      "We have recorded strings, flutes, choirs, brass and organs onto your computer the same way, with the same small faults that make these instruments sound alive: a little wow, a little flutter, and the scoop as the tape gets up to speed."
    ],
    "maker_note": [
      "My grandfather Arthur Hartwell opened the workshop in 1931 to repair harmoniums. My mother learned tape splicing on the parlour instruments that came in during the sixties. Ruth and I still keep them running.",
      "Play gently, and let each note finish before the tape runs out.",
      "Edith Hartwell, Hartwell & Daughters Ltd"
    ],
    "install": [
      "Insert the disc. In the Library tab it appears as TAPEPARLOUR.",
      "Open a folder (STRINGS, LEAD, VOCAL, BRASS, ORGAN, KEYS) and double-click a preset.",
      "The disc's own recordings are in SOUNDS; the handbook is TAPEPARL.PDF."
    ],
    "license": [
      "HARTWELL & DAUGHTERS LTD - LICENCE",
      "You may use the presets on one computer and use the music you make with them freely.",
      "Some recordings on this disc are by Paul Chauncy (Uncle Homunculus / Paulywauly), used with his permission; they may not be extracted or redistributed. See CREDITS.TXT.",
      "Hartwell & Daughters Ltd accept no liability for tape that has run out."
    ],
